{
  "requirement": "The `habeas-refactor` skill MUST tell agents not to edit evidence files by hand\nto follow a move, and that renaming a function or editing moved text is not a\nmove and needs reassessment.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement has two obligations for the `habeas-refactor` skill:\n1. It must tell agents not to hand-edit evidence files to follow a move.\n2. It must say that renaming a function, or editing moved text, is not a move and needs reassessment.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_refactor_skill_never_hand_edits_evidence_and_a_rename_is_not_a_move]` reads the real bundled `habeas-refactor` SKILL.md through `skill_md`. It isolates the `## Gotchas` section with `section`, which collapses the body so phrases that wrap across lines still match. `assert_text` then requires these phrases in that section:\n- \"Never edit evidence files by hand to follow a move\" covers the first obligation.\n- \"A rename is not a move. Renaming a function, type, or other item changes its code\" and \"its evidence needs reassessment\" cover the rename obligation and its reassessment consequence.\n- \"Editing moved code or text in the same step leaves every record that read it stale\" covers the editing-moved-text obligation.\n\n`assets/skills/habeas-refactor/SKILL.md` has a Gotchas section containing this text, so the assertions check the real content.\n\nThe test scopes its checks to the Gotchas section, so the phrases can't be satisfied by text elsewhere in the file. `assert_text` fails with the missing phrase if any one is absent. A skill that dropped the prohibition, or said a rename needs no reassessment, would fail the test.\n\nOne weak point: \"its evidence needs reassessment\" is checked as a separate phrase, not tied to the rename sentence. I don't count this as a gap, because it sits in the same section and the requirement does not demand that tight a binding. No other obligations or failure cases are named, and no acknowledgments apply.",
